      "created_at": "2019-01-07T15:53:41Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-01-07T15:53:41Z",
      "body": "> I work on confidential projects, where the thought of accidentally pushing to a GitHub remote is horrifying.\r\n\r\n@rgayler Could you please describe your use case some more so that we can better understand how to support your use of workflowr? For example, how are you sharing the workflowr website with your collaborators? Do you have a private web server or are you sending files over email? Did you use a remote Git hosting service of any kind, or do you plan to only use Git locally? How do you plan to share the code/data with your collaborators?\r\n\r\n> Would it be possible to add a local_only=TRUE argument to wflow_start() that takes steps to ensure that workflowr never tries to have dealings with a remote repo?\r\n\r\nSince this is the first request for this feature, I'm not ready to add an argument for it. For more complicated Git behavior, I think it is best to directly use Git (see below).\r\n\r\n> (Maybe a [git pre-push hook](https://blog.ghost.org/prevent-master-push/ could be used to prevent git from pushing to a remote.)\r\n\r\nI think this is the right solution. I saved the file below as `.git/hooks/pre-push`, made it executable with `chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-push`, and confirmed that it prevents pushing to a remote repository.\r\n\r\n```\r\n#!/bin/bash\r\n\r\necho \"This is a confidential project. Do not push the files to a remote server\"\r\necho \"For details, contact Ross Gayler via ...\"\r\nexit 1\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow this hook file is not transferred using the traditional Git commands (`push`, `pull`, `clone`), but I think that is ok for your use case since you don't seem to be using those commands. If you share the workflowr project directly with your collaborators (e.g. using a file sharing service or emailing a tarball), the pre-push hook will prevent them from pushing to GitHub or any other Git hosting website.",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
