      "created_at": "2019-01-09T00:21:02Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-01-09T00:21:02Z",
      "body": "**Use case:**\r\nMy current use case is that I am working on a project for a third party. The project is contractually constrained to be confidential - I can only share project materials with the third party.\r\nDuring the course of the project I need to repeatedly share results (but not code) for feedback.\r\nAt the end of the project the entire project directory is sent to the third party so they have the ability to reproduce and modify the analyses if they wish.\r\n\r\nI like the [Dat/Beakersuggestion](https://jdblischak.github.io/workflowr/articles/wflow-05-faq.html#can-i-share-workflowr-websites-directly-with-collaborators-in-a-secure-fashion) in the FAQ,\r\nbut the third-parties I work with tend to have a hard time getting anything non-standard into their IT environments.\r\n\r\nI use Git locally in the project directory to track changes to the code base. I protect against data loss with encrypted backup.\r\n\r\nI currently share the knitted results with the third party by sending an encrypted archive of the `docs` directory.\r\n\r\nAt the end of the project I will send an encrypted archive of the whole project directory to the third party.\r\n\r\nGeneralising my use case to more likely scientific scenarios, I think the key features are:\r\n\r\n1. The project contains or may generate information that must be kept confidential. This would be fairly common in any field that deals with human data, because there is a risk of having personally identifiable information. Even if you expect the analyses to only generate unidentifiable aggregate figures there is a risk that someone will accidentally slip some PII into the output text, e.g. when identifying an aberrant case that needs to be followed up (\"John Smith has negative age\").\r\n\r\n2. The project has collaborators who don't need to modify the code, e.g. subject-matter experts. They only need to review the outputs.\r\n\r\n> Since this is the first request for this feature, I'm not ready to add an argument for it.\r\n\r\nI think what I was after was firstly a declarative statement of intent to keep the project local. E.g. it could be a line in `_workflowr.yml` or the existence of a file named `CONFIDENTIAL.txt` in the project directory. Then given the declaration that the project is not to be published (unintentionally) any mechanisms to prevent accidental publication via Git are essentially implementation detail.\r\n\r\n> I think [a Git pre-push hook] is the right solution.\r\n\r\nSo the suggestion was that something like that might be built in to `workflowr` so that users who know almost nothing about Git (e.g. me) might have more confidence in using `workflowr` for confidential projects.",
      "author_association": "NONE",
      "pin": null,
