      "created_at": "2019-01-09T22:48:34Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-01-09T22:48:34Z",
      "body": "@rgayler Thanks for the explanation. It is very insightful to learn the various ways that workflowr is being used.\r\n\r\n> At the end of the project I will send an encrypted archive of the whole project directory to the third party.\r\n\r\nGreat. This means that the pre-push hook is a good solution for this situation.\r\n\r\n> The project contains or may generate information that must be kept confidential.\r\n\r\nI agree this is a common situation. My problem trying to generalize it is that disabling Git is not necessarily required in this situation. For example, if the organization uses [GitHub Enterprise](https://github.com/enterprise), then the analysts can push to the remote repository even though the data is confidential.\r\n\r\n> it could be a line in _workflowr.yml or the existence of a file named CONFIDENTIAL.txt in the project directory.\r\n\r\nI think adding such a file is best done manually. It's not as common as say a README.md file.\r\n\r\n> So the suggestion was that something like that might be built in to workflowr so that users who know almost nothing about Git (e.g. me) might have more confidence in using workflowr for confidential projects.\r\n\r\nHow about an argument to `wflow_start()` named something like `disable_remote`? When set to `TRUE`, it would create the file `.git/hooks/pre-push` and make it executable. I'd propose the file contain something like the following (suggestions welcome):\r\n\r\n```\r\n#!/bin/bash\r\n\r\n# This hook prevents pushing to a remote repository, which is ideal for a\r\n# project containing confidential data. It was created by wflow_start() with the\r\n# argument disable_remote = TRUE. If you decide you want to be able to push this\r\n# repository, delete this file.\r\n\r\necho \"This is a confidential project. Do not push the files to a remote server\"\r\nexit 1\r\n```\r\n",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
