      "created_at": "2019-01-10T02:18:58Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-01-10T02:18:58Z",
      "body": "> if the organization uses GitHub Enterprise, then the analysts can push to the remote repository even though the data is confidential.\r\n\r\n1. So the core concept is \"not to be pushed to a public repo\" (which may follow from confidentiality).\r\n2. The feature is aimed at users who are not technically sophisticated (or have access to technical sophistication).\r\n\r\n> wflow_start() named something like disable_remote? When set to TRUE, it would create the file .git/hooks/pre-push\r\n\r\nThat sounds great. The existence of the argument makes it clear that the possibility exists and absolves the technically naive user of having to come up with a technical solution. I just redefined `wflow_git_remote()` and `wflow_git_push()` to give an error message, but the pre-push hook prevents pushing by any mechanism. The mechanism doesn't have to be absolutely watertight (a malign actor can always find a workaround) it just needs to give a degree of well-founded confidence that the user is unlikely to accidentally expose the project.",
